============================================================================================================
3.  WHICH MODELS DEPEND ON pi AT ALL?
============================================================================================================
            model  uses_pi  eps_true_pi  eps_wrong_pi   shift
   conversion_glm    False      +2.0157       +2.0157 +0.0000
conversion_gbm_v2    False      +3.1529       +3.1529 +0.0000
    arm_posterior     True      +2.1957       +2.3305 +0.1348

  Only the arm-posterior family reads pi.  The GLM and the GBM take the price as a feature
  and never touch the allocation, so a misspecified pi cannot hurt them -- which, given how
  uncertain the 0.1/0.8/0.1 figure is, is a point in their favour that had not been counted.

============================================================================================================
4.  THE REALISTIC FAILURE: pi VARIES BY SEGMENT AND IS ASSUMED CONSTANT
============================================================================================================
Staged rollouts, channel-specific test rates and business overrides all produce
this.  Here the test is run at 0.2/0.6/0.2 on aggregator traffic and 0.02/0.96/0.02
elsewhere, then analysed as if it were the pooled average.

  realised pooled allocation: [0.1197 0.7627 0.1176]

                     handling  mean_eps    bias    rmse
    assume the pooled average   +1.6734 -0.4508 +2.0126
assume the design 0.1/0.8/0.1   +1.7976 -0.3266 +1.9885
 use the true pi_k(x) per row   +1.8421 -0.2821 +1.9258

  A constant pi is not a harmless approximation when the test was not run uniformly.  The
  fix is cheap and exact -- record pi_k(x) per quote at assignment time and carry it -- but
  it has to be DESIGNED IN.  Reconstructing it afterwards from realised shares is precisely
  the pooled-average row.

